1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:11,614
In November, Tomás Reyes taught Albert
Wesker the internet.

2
00:00:12,183 --> 00:00:18,249
It began by accident. The Director had come into the
break room at lunchtime for coffee,

3
00:00:18,269 --> 00:00:24,128
and found Tomás scrolling, and asked him — in the
tone of a man asking about a rash —

4
00:00:24,148 --> 00:00:25,719
what he was looking at.

5
00:00:26,410 --> 00:00:31,396
It's just — it's a guy who restores old knives. He
finds them in rivers. Like, rusty.

6
00:00:31,416 --> 00:00:32,750
And then he fixes them.

7
00:00:33,271 --> 00:00:35,448
How many people are watching him fix them?

8
00:00:35,968 --> 00:00:37,334
This one? Uh. Four million.

9
00:00:38,454 --> 00:00:41,910
Four million people. Watching a
stranger clean a knife.

10
00:00:42,432 --> 00:00:43,984
I mean, it's really satisfying.

11
00:00:44,503 --> 00:00:46,066
The Director sat down.

12
00:00:46,636 --> 00:00:52,708
He did not do that. In two months, nobody had seen
him sit in the break room. He sat down

13
00:00:52,728 --> 00:00:56,611
across from Tomás, and took off his
sunglasses, and said:

14
00:00:57,301 --> 00:00:58,368
Show me everything.

15
00:00:59,889 --> 00:01:01,414
So Tomás showed him.

16
00:01:01,984 --> 00:01:08,122
Short videos, ninety seconds long, served by a machine
that learned what you wanted before

17
00:01:08,142 --> 00:01:14,007
you did. Feeds that never ended. Comment sections
where strangers who would never meet

18
00:01:14,027 --> 00:01:19,960
decided, in their thousands, who was a hero and who
was a villain before lunch. Men who

19
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:25,913
had become famous for being angry. Women who had
become famous for being calm. Children

20
00:01:25,933 --> 00:01:32,208
who had become famous for nothing at all. Numbers
everywhere — views and likes and followers

21
00:01:32,228 --> 00:01:38,024
and shares — ticking upward like the odometer of a
car driven by the whole human race

22
00:01:38,044 --> 00:01:38,572
at once.

23
00:01:40,061 --> 00:01:40,959
Oh.

24
00:01:41,478 --> 00:01:46,558
It was a very small word. Wesker had never
heard that voice make it before.

25
00:01:47,248 --> 00:01:53,677
They have built it. They have built the machine. A
rally that never ends. A crowd in every

26
00:01:53,697 --> 00:01:56,401
pocket. And no one is in charge of it.

27
00:01:56,921 --> 00:02:01,232
Someone's always in charge of it. They just
don't stand on a balcony.

28
00:02:01,751 --> 00:02:08,702
I spent a year of my life writing speeches for a
radio set in every kitchen. This — this

29
00:02:08,722 --> 00:02:12,504
is a radio set in every hand,
that listens back.

30
00:02:14,025 --> 00:02:16,186
You okay, Doc? You've gone kind of still.

31
00:02:16,706 --> 00:02:18,435
How does it decide what to show them?

32
00:02:18,956 --> 00:02:23,926
Honestly? Whatever makes them feel something. Angry
works best. Then scared. Then, like,

33
00:02:23,946 --> 00:02:25,966
awe. Anything with awe does numbers.

34
00:02:27,086 --> 00:02:29,512
The Director put his sunglasses back on.

35
00:02:30,603 --> 00:02:32,033
You know you do that thing, right?

36
00:02:32,552 --> 00:02:33,334
What thing?

37
00:02:33,855 --> 00:02:38,212
The — okay, don't be mad. On the internet it's
called aura farming. When somebody does

38
00:02:38,232 --> 00:02:42,690
something so effortlessly cool that everyone watching
kind of loses their mind a little.

39
00:02:42,710 --> 00:02:47,015
Like, the way you just sat down and took the
glasses off and said show me everything.

40
00:02:47,035 --> 00:02:51,187
That's aura. You're farming it. Constantly. It's
actually kind of insane to watch.

41
00:02:51,707 --> 00:02:53,031
And aura can be farmed.

42
00:02:53,551 --> 00:02:55,152
I mean — it's a joke. Kind of.

43
00:02:55,673 --> 00:02:59,599
It is called charisma. We had it
before the telephone.

44
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:03,528
They've turned it into a number. How
very efficient of them.

45
00:03:05,549 --> 00:03:09,902
The Shop had a blueprint that week that he had
been watching since October.

46
00:03:11,223 --> 00:03:14,186
Blueprint. Rapid pathogen assay.

47
00:03:21,918 --> 00:03:27,756
It was not a weapon. It was not a power. It was a
small plastic cartridge and a reader

48
00:03:27,776 --> 00:03:33,887
the size of a paperback, that could tell a nurse in a
rural clinic what was making a child

49
00:03:33,907 --> 00:03:39,200
sick in the time it took to make tea — and it
did not exist anywhere on Earth.

50
00:03:39,890 --> 00:03:43,475
A medical device. You are buying
a thermometer.

51
00:03:43,996 --> 00:03:50,266
I'm buying a face. Every empire needs one. Yours had a
uniform. Mine will have a hospital.

52
00:03:51,417 --> 00:03:52,858
Purchase confirmed.

53
00:03:57,206 --> 00:04:03,032
The documentation arrived the way Pell had arrived —
as if it had always been there. A

54
00:04:03,052 --> 00:04:08,878
folder on the division's server, dated across
eighteen months, containing the complete

55
00:04:08,898 --> 00:04:14,791
design history of a device that nobody in the
building had designed. Protocols. Reagent

56
00:04:14,811 --> 00:04:20,773
lists. Failure analyses. Two rejected prototypes. Lab
notebooks, scanned, in handwriting

57
00:04:20,793 --> 00:04:25,667
that matched, with unnerving precision, the
handwriting of Evelyn Marsh.

58
00:04:27,737 --> 00:04:29,500
He gave it to her that evening.

59
00:04:30,069 --> 00:04:36,207
She was at her bench, at seven, alone, running the
same sequencing job she had been running

60
00:04:36,227 --> 00:04:42,025
for three weeks. He put a binder down beside her
keyboard, and when she opened it, and

61
00:04:42,045 --> 00:04:45,814
saw her own handwriting on page one,
she went very pale.

62
00:04:46,504 --> 00:04:48,276
This is — I didn't write this.

63
00:04:48,797 --> 00:04:49,417
No.

64
00:04:49,937 --> 00:04:55,801
It's my hand. It's my notation. I use that little
arrow for rate-limiting steps. Nobody

65
00:04:55,821 --> 00:04:58,237
else does that. I didn't write this.

66
00:04:58,757 --> 00:05:00,913
I know. It's still yours. If you want it.

67
00:05:02,432 --> 00:05:07,902
She read for a while without speaking. She was a good
enough scientist to understand what

68
00:05:07,922 --> 00:05:13,330
she was reading, and to understand that it worked,
and to understand that nothing in her

69
00:05:13,350 --> 00:05:17,463
education could explain how it had come to
be sitting on her bench.

70
00:05:18,033 --> 00:05:23,474
She could have asked. A more careful person would
have. Nadia Okafor would have asked

71
00:05:23,494 --> 00:05:26,109
forty questions and then called a lawyer.

72
00:05:26,677 --> 00:05:32,304
Evelyn Marsh closed the binder, and put both hands
flat on the cover, and looked up at

73
00:05:32,324 --> 00:05:32,567
him.

74
00:05:33,257 --> 00:05:34,235
You said not yet.

75
00:05:34,755 --> 00:05:35,461
I did.

76
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:36,728
Is it now?

77
00:05:38,050 --> 00:05:38,819
It's now.

78
00:05:40,538 --> 00:05:45,770
It is worth recording what she felt, because it
mattered later. It was not gratitude.

79
00:05:45,790 --> 00:05:51,208
Gratitude is something you feel toward an equal. It
was not quite love, though it looked

80
00:05:51,228 --> 00:05:56,583
like it from the outside. It was the thing people
feel in cathedrals — the dizzy, total

81
00:05:56,603 --> 00:06:01,959
surrender of a person who has spent her whole life
believing she was alone in the dark,

82
00:06:01,979 --> 00:06:07,334
and has suddenly been shown that someone was
watching, and that the someone had a plan.

83
00:06:07,904 --> 00:06:10,892
It was, in other words, the
beginning of a faith.

84
00:06:12,963 --> 00:06:14,810
The speech happened on a Friday.

85
00:06:15,379 --> 00:06:21,022
It was not planned as a speech. He had called
everyone into the break room at a quarter

86
00:06:21,042 --> 00:06:26,554
to five to tell them about the assay — all of them,
the six who had been there before

87
00:06:26,574 --> 00:06:32,086
him and the three who had arrived since. Dana,
Lessing, Evelyn, Tomás, Nadia, Walter,

88
00:06:32,106 --> 00:06:37,748
who had come in early for it. Pell, standing
perfectly still by the fridge. Gus, by the

89
00:06:37,768 --> 00:06:39,701
door. Hale, by the other door.

90
00:06:40,271 --> 00:06:46,235
He had meant to talk for two minutes. He talked for
five. He did not use notes. Afterwards,

91
00:06:46,255 --> 00:06:52,089
none of them could remember exactly what he had said,
only how it had felt, and when they

92
00:06:52,109 --> 00:06:57,745
tried to explain it to their families, they sounded,
even to themselves, a little mad.

93
00:06:58,435 --> 00:07:03,553
Two months ago, you were told something about
yourselves. You were told it by a letter

94
00:07:03,573 --> 00:07:08,631
from London, and by a frozen bank account, and by
every empty chair in this building.

95
00:07:08,651 --> 00:07:11,439
You were told that you were what
was left over.

96
00:07:12,240 --> 00:07:18,474
The leftovers of a dead company. In a building
nobody visits. In a town nobody writes

97
00:07:18,494 --> 00:07:21,565
songs about. Doing work that nobody reads.

98
00:07:22,086 --> 00:07:23,264
He let that sit.

99
00:07:23,954 --> 00:07:29,791
I'd like you to remember that feeling. Not because it
was true. Because every great thing

100
00:07:29,811 --> 00:07:35,648
that has ever happened in this world began in a room
exactly like this one. A room nobody

101
00:07:35,668 --> 00:07:39,004
was watching. With a few people
nobody had counted.

102
00:07:39,904 --> 00:07:45,150
The men in glass buildings across that river don't
believe that. That's why they're going

103
00:07:45,170 --> 00:07:45,978
to lose to us.

104
00:07:47,298 --> 00:07:53,251
His voice had not risen. That was the uncanny thing.
It had not risen at all. But something

105
00:07:53,271 --> 00:07:59,093
in the rhythm of it had changed — a cadence, a pulse,
a way of stepping from one sentence

106
00:07:59,113 --> 00:08:04,738
to the next that made each one feel like a stair.
And behind his eyes, in the quiet of

107
00:08:04,758 --> 00:08:10,579
the skull, the other half of him had leaned forward,
the way an old musician leans toward

108
00:08:10,599 --> 00:08:13,795
an instrument he has not touched
in eighty years.

109
00:08:14,485 --> 00:08:19,828
In twelve months, there will be a device in hospitals
that tells a doctor what is killing

110
00:08:19,848 --> 00:08:25,251
a child in twelve minutes. It will have our name on
it. Not Verdant's. Not London's. Ours.

111
00:08:25,271 --> 00:08:30,794
Umbrella. And every nurse who uses it will know that it
came from here — from this building,

112
00:08:30,814 --> 00:08:32,300
from this room, from you.

113
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:38,387
That is the first thing. It is not the last thing. I
will not tell you tonight what the

114
00:08:38,407 --> 00:08:43,754
last thing is, because you would not believe me. But
I will tell you this. I did not buy

115
00:08:43,774 --> 00:08:45,584
this company to keep it alive.

116
00:08:46,783 --> 00:08:48,576
I bought it to make it inevitable.

117
00:08:50,597 --> 00:08:56,552
Tomás was the first on his feet. Then Dana. Then,
to his own surprise, Walter Pruitt,

118
00:08:56,572 --> 00:09:02,738
who had sworn on the day he took off the uniform
never to stand up for an officer again.

119
00:09:03,307 --> 00:09:09,136
Evelyn did not clap. She was crying, without seeming
to notice, her hands pressed together

120
00:09:09,156 --> 00:09:10,565
in front of her mouth.

121
00:09:11,135 --> 00:09:17,670
Pell, Gus, and Hale clapped in perfect unison, exactly
the same number of times, and stopped

122
00:09:17,690 --> 00:09:22,443
at exactly the same moment. Nobody
noticed. They were all too busy.

123
00:09:24,014 --> 00:09:26,266
Nadia Okafor did not clap.

124
00:09:26,836 --> 00:09:32,447
She stood at the back with her arms folded, and she
watched the room. Not the man at the

125
00:09:32,467 --> 00:09:38,014
front — the room. The faces. The way the applause
had come not from agreement, but from

126
00:09:38,034 --> 00:09:43,453
something underneath agreement. She had seen that
look before, once, a long time ago,

127
00:09:43,473 --> 00:09:49,020
at a church revival her grandmother had taken her to
in Lagos, when a preacher had made

128
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:52,668
a whole tent full of sensible people
fall to their knees.

129
00:09:53,238 --> 00:09:58,868
It had frightened her then. It frightened her more
now, because this time there was no

130
00:09:58,888 --> 00:10:04,585
God in the room. Only a man in a black shirt,
standing very still, with his hand on the

131
00:10:04,605 --> 00:10:06,162
back of a plastic chair.

132
00:10:08,981 --> 00:10:14,668
Assessment. Personnel morale, fervent.
Four thousand Points.

133
00:10:16,889 --> 00:10:18,970
Level up. Level five.

134
00:10:21,193 --> 00:10:27,029
Four thousand. For five minutes of talking in a break
room. More than the System had paid

135
00:10:27,049 --> 00:10:32,753
him for the building, or the landlord, or the
traitor. More than everything else he had

136
00:10:32,773 --> 00:10:34,859
done in two months put together.

137
00:10:35,429 --> 00:10:41,212
The Wesker half of the mind noted the number, and
filed it, the way an investor files

138
00:10:41,232 --> 00:10:47,015
a return. The Adolf half did not note it at all. It
was elsewhere. It was remembering

139
00:10:47,035 --> 00:10:52,886
beer halls, and stadiums, and the roar of a crowd
coming back at it like surf — and it

140
00:10:52,906 --> 00:10:56,914
was, for the first time in this new
life, completely happy.

141
00:10:58,403 --> 00:10:59,770
Two of six.

142
00:11:00,291 --> 00:11:01,146
Which two?

143
00:11:01,664 --> 00:11:03,351
You know which two.

144
00:11:05,501 --> 00:11:06,977
Command presence.

145
00:11:12,371 --> 00:11:14,515
He bought it before he left the building.

146
00:11:15,836 --> 00:11:17,278
Purchase confirmed.

147
00:11:21,461 --> 00:11:27,087
At seven twenty the next morning, Tomás Reyes called
the Director's cell phone for the

148
00:11:27,107 --> 00:11:28,597
first time in his life.

149
00:11:29,287 --> 00:11:33,633
Okay, so, please don't kill me. I kind of filmed a bit
of it. Yesterday. Just, like, twenty

150
00:11:33,653 --> 00:11:37,855
seconds. The bit about the room nobody was watching.
And I put it on my page, which has,

151
00:11:37,875 --> 00:11:42,029
like, four hundred people, and I went to bed, and —
Doc, it's got forty thousand views.

152
00:11:42,049 --> 00:11:45,100
It's got forty thousand views and people
are asking who you are.

153
00:11:46,619 --> 00:11:48,917
The Director did not answer immediately.

154
00:11:49,606 --> 00:11:53,597
Forty thousand. In one night.
From a break room.

155
00:11:54,116 --> 00:11:55,461
Take it down, Tomás.

156
00:11:55,982 --> 00:11:59,397
Yeah. Yeah, totally. I'm sorry, I'm —
taking it down now. I'm sorry.

157
00:11:59,918 --> 00:12:02,884
Don't be sorry. It was good work.
It was just early.

158
00:12:04,004 --> 00:12:04,586
Early?

159
00:12:05,105 --> 00:12:06,643
Keep the file. We'll need it.

160
00:12:08,363 --> 00:12:14,103
Tomás took it down. By then it had been downloaded
two hundred times, and three of those

161
00:12:14,123 --> 00:12:19,930
copies would still be circulating on the internet
eleven years later, in a very different

162
00:12:19,950 --> 00:12:25,495
world, under titles like The First Speech, and
Where It Started, and Watch His Hands.

163
00:12:27,065 --> 00:12:32,782
In lab two, at two in the morning, Evelyn Marsh was
building the first prototype of the

164
00:12:32,802 --> 00:12:38,585
assay with her own hands, from a set of instructions
in her own handwriting that she had

165
00:12:38,605 --> 00:12:39,509
never written.

166
00:12:40,080 --> 00:12:45,928
She was humming as she worked. Four notes. Falling,
and falling, and falling, and landing

167
00:12:45,948 --> 00:12:47,312
somewhere unresolved.

168
00:12:47,883 --> 00:12:49,893
She did not know she was doing it.
